---
description: Find and resolve duplicates in a target/module, diff [base], or staged changes
---

Treat `$ARGUMENTS` as the requested scope and options, not a Beads scope. Use these invocation forms:

| Invocation | Mode and scope |
| --- | --- |
| `/dry-code <module_name>` | Target mode; scan only the requested path(s). Use the repository root only when the whole project is explicitly requested. |
| `/dry-code diff [<base_branch>]` | Compare a selected worker worktree against the supplied base, defaulting to `main` when omitted; include its committed and uncommitted work. |
| `/dry-code staged` | Staged-only mode; include only staged files and hunks, never unstaged changes. |

Use the actual selected Git diff and its changed-line ranges; do not equate a diff with Git status. In staged-only mode, do not inspect diff ranges until the empty-staged check below passes. Ask only for other missing or ambiguous inputs, not for the diff base when `main` is the default.

In diff mode, resolve the target before scanning. First use this session's recorded worker launches and worktree paths; prefer its most recently used suitable worker, and do not enumerate unrelated workspaces. Only if none of those workers is suitable, discover other worker worktrees for this repository from available worker/Herdr metadata and linked-worktree information—not branch-name lookup or a workspace-wide scan. Exclude the invoking/reference checkout and any non-worker worktree. A suitable worker must be a distinct linked checkout for this repository with changes against the resolved base; never silently fall back to the invoking checkout or scan it. If no suitable worker exists, say so and stop. If a worker must be selected, use the question tool once to offer the suitable workers by worker/workspace name (and path when useful), recommending the most recently used suitable worker according to available workspace activity metadata; do not ask for a branch name. If worker and base genuinely both need user input, ask both in this same questionnaire. An omitted base is `main` and is not a question; honor any supplied or selected base exactly, and stop with an error if it cannot be resolved. Inspect the selected checkout in place using read-only operations only: do not switch branches, alter its index or files, or move/modify the invoking checkout. Preserve all existing user changes.

In staged-only mode, first run a read-only staged-diff check, such as `git diff --cached --quiet --exit-code`, before the test gate, diff-range extraction, snapshot creation, jscpd, or any scan delegation. Treat exit 0 as no staged changes: clearly say there are no staged changes to compare and stop successfully without approval or edits. Treat exit 1 as staged changes and continue; report any other check failure as an error and stop, never as an empty diff. The index is authoritative, including partially staged files. Build an isolated temporary scan snapshot from the index and use staged diff ranges; never scan working-tree content as staged content or inspect unstaged changes as staged work. Compare against the rest of that index snapshot when needed for unchanged-file matches. If an index-only snapshot or scan cannot be safely produced, stop and report rather than falling back to the worktree. Preserve the real index and all user modifications: do not stage, unstage, reset, or overwrite worktree content. Apply approved edits only when they can be safely confined to the approved staged-code locations without disturbing unstaged work; otherwise stop and report that safe staged-only execution is not possible.

At invocation, provide an explicit choice to include or exclude tests; if the user has not specified it, ask before scanning. This gate is language-agnostic. When tests are included, favor readable, self-contained test stories over extracting helpers solely to reduce duplication. When tests are excluded, identify test files and directories conservatively using repository conventions and exclude them from detection where practical, without broadly suppressing production code. If using `jscpd --ignore`, preserve every existing managed ignore entry because this option replaces the config's ignore list; add only narrow test exclusions. Do not assume an `--include-tests` option changes clone detection. When tests are excluded, discard any remaining findings in test code during assessment, including inline test modules; for mixed production/test files, ignore only test-code locations, not production code. When tests are included, test-code findings remain eligible for assessment, with readability favored over extracting helpers solely to deduplicate.

Run local jscpd with the managed config and compact reporter on the resolved target or chosen comparison corpus:

```sh
jscpd --config "$HOME/.config/opencode/jscpd.json" --reporters ai --no-tips --fail-on-empty -- "<resolved-target-or-corpus>"
```

Replace the placeholder with the resolved path or corpus; for multiple paths, pass each as a separate quoted argument. In diff mode, run the comparison from the selected worker checkout against the resolved base: include committed and tracked staged/unstaged changes (for example, the base-to-worktree diff), and include untracked files as added files with their full line ranges. Use these actual changed-line ranges, retaining only candidates with a duplicate location intersecting them. jscpd does not filter clones by changed lines, so assess its reported locations against the diff. To find duplication between changed and unchanged project code, include an appropriate wider comparison corpus containing both; scanning only changed files cannot find unchanged-file matches. Report the actual corpus, exclusions and limitations, and never claim full-project coverage when only changed files were scanned. Do not use `jscpd --changed` (it follows Git status and may write `.jscpd-baseline.json`) or `--changed-only` (it misses unchanged-file matches); avoid unexpected baseline or worktree side effects. Keep local-only detection: do not substitute project configuration, use MCP or remote analysis services, or enable embeddings or downloads.

Read reported source fragments and nearby context to assess whether each finding is genuinely equivalent and worth simplifying. Report clone locations, actual scope and material limitations, including excluded code, unavailable cross-target matches, scan failures, empty analysis or truncated output. Do not treat incomplete results as a complete scan.

For each candidate, assign a 1–3 value/feasibility score: 3 means a clear, worthwhile change; 2 means a genuine unresolved trade-off that needs user or architectural judgment; 1 means leave it alone, and ignore it for execution. Explain the score briefly. Preserve intentional repetition and never turn superficial similarity into an abstraction.

Omit score-1 findings from approval. Before asking, present the number of score-3 findings in a brief paragraph; retain their individual evidence and details for precise execution briefs. If there are score-2 findings, show them in a table with columns `Index`, `Affected code`, `Trade-off`, and `Recommendation`; for each finding, give concrete individual file paths and line numbers or ranges for every duplicate location. Do not compress paths into brace notation without individual line references. Describe the genuine trade-off requiring judgment and include a recommendation where useful. Do not show a score-1 finding, and omit the table when there are no score-2 findings.

If there are no score-2 or score-3 findings, report that nothing worthwhile is approved and stop without asking or editing. Otherwise, use the question tool exactly once for one questionnaire containing all applicable questions—never use sequential questionnaires: (1) yes/no approval of all score-3 findings, when any exist; (2) one concise score-2 question using the question tool's built-in free-form answer, asking for `all`, `none`, or selected table indices (for example, `1, 2, 5`), when any exist; and (3) waves or one batch, with a recommendation, only when the number and relationships of findings make that choice meaningful. Do not create a separate option for each score-2 finding. If the index answer is ambiguous, approve no score-2 finding rather than guessing. A single-batch choice still requires safe ordering for dependencies and deliberate handling of overlapping code. Respect each answer independently, including declining all score-3 findings while selecting score-2 findings. Execute only the approved findings; if none are approved, stop without editing.

For approved work, create self-contained, score-free execution briefs identifying the concrete duplication, affected files/code, intended outcome, and relevant constraints; do not mention score categories or depend on the assessment context. If waves were selected and warranted, group the briefs into manageable waves accounting for overlap and dependencies. Implement only within the requested target; in diff mode, make approved edits in the selected worker worktree, never in the invoking checkout. Then summarize changes and remaining findings. Never execute score-1 findings or unselected score-2 findings.
